b18"] [17592186231454 :knowledge-point/questions "question-dc43d95b-1383-587c-8ba1-6694a0612d5c"] [17592186232043 :tutorial/content "In a previous lesson, we learned that $\\max$ and $\\min$ return the largest and smallest *function values,* while $\\arg\\max$ and $\\arg\\min$ return the corresponding *input values.*\n\nIn this lesson, we practice finding $\\max,$ $\\arg\\max,$ $\\min,$ and $\\arg\\min$ from common representations of a function:\n\n- Tables of values\n\n- Graphs"] [17592186232044 :tutorial/content "When a table lists input-output pairs $(x,f(x))$ for $x$ in some set $S,$ we can read:\n\n- $\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)$ by finding the largest output value in the table.\n\n- $\\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)$ by taking the input value where that largest output occurs.\n\nThe same idea applies to $\\min$ and $\\arg\\min,$ except we look for the smallest output value."] [17592186232045 :tutorial/content "When a graph shows the points $(x,f(x))$ for $x$ in a set $S,$ we can find:\n\n- $\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)$ by locating the highest point on the graph and reading its $y$-coordinate.\n\n- $\\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)$ by taking the corresponding $x$-coordinate.\n\nThe same idea applies to $\\min$ and $\\arg\\min,$ except we look for the lowest point."] [17592186231552 :activity/expected-seconds 480.0]] "retractions" [[17592186231379 :question/problem "| $x$ | $f(x)$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $-2$ | $3$ |\n| $-1$ | $0$ |\n| $0$ | $5$ |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ |\n\nThe table above gives values of $f$ on the set $S=\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}$. Use the table to compute\n$\\max_{x \\in S} f(x),\\qquad \\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x),\\qquad \\min_{x \\in S} f(x),\\qquad \\arg\\min_{x \\in S} f(x)$."] [17592186231379 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n- $\\max\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is the maximum output value, while\n- $\\arg\\max\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is an input in $S$ where that maximum output occurs.\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n- $\\min\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is the minimum output value, while\n- $\\arg\\min\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is an input in $S$ where that minimum output occurs.\n\nThe output values in the table are $3,0,5,-1$, and $2$.\n\nThe largest output is $5$, and it occurs at $x=0$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)=5 \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)=0\n$$\n\nThe smallest output is $-1$, and it occurs at $x=1$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\min_{x \\in S} f(x)=-1 \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\arg\\min_{x \\in S} f(x)=1\n$$"] [17592186231380 :question/problem "![](images/eb/eb6079ac70816cd56061b8b353cc734176eed1698c50376a2e7a34c40f582a89.png)\n\nLet $S=\\{0,1,2,3,4\\}$. The graph above shows the value of $f(x)$ for each $x \\in S$. Use the graph to compute\n$\\max_{x \\in S} f(x),\\qquad \\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x),\\qquad \\min_{x \\in S} f(x),\\qquad \\arg\\min_{x \\in S} f(x)$."] [17592186231380 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n- $\\max\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is the maximum output value, while\n- $\\arg\\max\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is an input in $S$ where that maximum output occurs.\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n- $\\min\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is the minimum output value, while\n- $\\arg\\min\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is an input in $S$ where that minimum output occurs.\n\nFrom the graph, the highest point has $y$-coordinate $5$, and it occurs at $x=1$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)=\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{5} \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)=\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{1}\n$$\n\nThe lowest point has $y$-coordinate $0$, and it occurs at $x=4$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\min_{x \\in S} f(x)=\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{0} \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\arg\\min_{x \\in S} f(x)=\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{4}\n$$"] [17592186231740 :question/problem "| $t$ | $h(t)$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $-3$ | $2$ |\n| $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $0$ | $-5$ |\n| $2$ | $4$ |\n| $5$ | $0$ |\n\nThe table above gives values of a function $h$ on the set $T = {-3,-1, 0, 2, 5}$. Compute the following:\n\n$max_(t ∈ T)h(t) =$\n{{field-1}} $\\arg max_(t ∈ T)h(t) =$\n{{field-2}} $min_(t ∈ T)h(t) =$\n{{field-3}} $\\arg min_(t ∈ T)h(t) =$\n{{field-4}}"] [17592186231741 :question/problem "![](images/af/af893cca4e4fe3d4b62f02997fa799dec3e35fbe8942d2d3c90d2eeb6bbc919c.png)\n\nLet $T = {-3,-2, 0, 1, 3}$. The graph above shows the value of $g(x)$ for each $x ∈ T$. Assuming that all the dots have integer coordinates, compute the following:\n\n$max_(x ∈ T)g(x) =$\n{{field-1}} $\\arg max_(x ∈ T)g(x) =$\n{{field-2}} $min_(x ∈ T)g(x) =$\n{{field-3}} $\\arg min_(x ∈ T)g(x) =$\n{{field-4}}"] [17592186231739 :question/problem "![](images/68/68b36fbb370ee2d0fe18ad08d0864ef9d127abded1fc6d1c113009cd865feb1b.png)\n\nLet $T = {-1, 0, 1, 2, 3}$. The graph above shows the value of $h(x)$ for each $x ∈ T$. Assuming that all the dots have integer coordinates, compute the following:\n\n$max_(x ∈ T)h(x) =$\n{{field-1}} $\\arg max_(x ∈ T)h(x) =$\n{{field-2}} $min_(x ∈ T)h(x) =$\n{{field-3}} $\\arg min_(x ∈ T)h(x) =$\n{{field-4}}"] [17592186232043 :tutorial/content "In a previous lesson, we learned that $\\max$ and $\\min$ return the largest and smallest *function values,* while $\\arg\\max$ and $\\arg\\min$ return the corresponding *input values.*\n\nIn this lesson, we practice finding $\\max$, $\\arg\\max$, $\\min$, and $\\arg\\min$ from common representations of a function:\n\n- Tables of values\n- Graphs"] [17592186232044 :tutorial/content "When a table lists input-output pairs $(x,f(x))$ for $x$ in some set $S$, we can read:\n\n- $\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)$ by finding the largest output value in the table.\n- $\\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)$ by taking the input value where that largest output occurs.\n\nThe same idea applies to $\\min$ and $\\arg\\min$, except we look for the smallest output value."] [17592186232045 :tutorial/content "When a graph shows the points $(x,f(x))$ for $x$ in a set $S$, we can find:\n\n- $\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)$ by locating the highest point on the graph and reading its $y$-coordinate.\n- $\\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)$ by taking the corresponding $x$-coordinate.\n\nThe same idea applies to $\\min$ and $\\arg\\min$, except we look for the lowest point."]]}]